  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that 4-Prop-2-Enoxychromen-2-One may be involved in the regulation of the Inner centromere protein through multiple indirect pathways. The drug compound is implicated in targeting the Werner syndrome ATP-dependent helicase, which in turn is a target of Excisusin B, and both of these are ultimately linked to the Inner centromere protein. Additionally, the compound is also associated with targeting Multidrug resistance-associated protein 7, which is a target of 6-Methoxysorigenin, both of which also lead to the Inner centromere protein. These findings collectively indicate that 4-Prop-2-Enoxychromen-2-One may act as a modulator of the Inner centromere protein through different molecular mechanisms, possibly by influencing helicase activity or other cellular processes. The evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, suggesting a potential role for the compound in the regulation of centromere function, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
